Assignment 5 - Network traffic monitoring with libpcap
======================================================

Name: Kyriakos Chalvatzis
AM:   2018030043

Files
-----
pcap_ex.c   the monitor
makefile    build (links -lpcap)
README      this file

Build & run
-----------
  make
  ./pcap_ex -r test_pcap_5mins.pcap            # read a file, print to terminal
  ./pcap_ex -i eth0                            # live capture, output to log.txt
  ./pcap_ex -i eth0 -f "port 8080"             # live capture with a filter
  ./pcap_ex -r test_pcap_5mins.pcap -f "udp"   # filter an offline read

With -i the output goes to log.txt; with -r it goes to the terminal.
Stop a live capture with Ctrl-C; the statistics are printed on exit.

Implementation
--------------
Packets are decoded by hand:
  Ethernet (14 bytes) -> IPv4 (struct ip) or IPv6 (struct ip6_hdr) -> TCP/UDP.
Non-IP and non-TCP/UDP packets are counted but skipped. For each TCP/UDP
packet the program prints: protocol, source and destination IP, source and
destination port, the L4 header length, the payload length and the address of
the payload in memory.

Filtering (manual, no pcap_compile/pcap_setfilter)
--------------------------------------------------
The -f expression is parsed by hand and matched per packet. Supported,
AND-combined:
  tcp | udp
  [src|dst] port <n>
  [src|dst] host <ip>          (IPv4 or IPv6)
e.g. "udp", "port 443", "tcp dst port 80", "host 192.168.3.131".

Flows and statistics
--------------------
A flow is the 5-tuple {src IP, src port, dst IP, dst port, protocol}, kept in a
hash table with chaining so each packet is O(1). On exit the program prints:
total/TCP/UDP flows, total/TCP/UDP packets received (including skipped ones in
the total), and total TCP/UDP bytes (on-wire length).

Retransmissions (questions 11-13)
---------------------------------
TCP: yes, we can flag likely retransmissions. Per flow we track the highest
sequence number seen so far (next_seq). A data segment whose whole range
(seq .. seq+len) lies at or before next_seq carries only bytes we have already
seen, so it is marked "[Retransmitted]". This is a heuristic - it works from
sequence numbers alone, without reassembling the stream.

UDP: no. UDP has no sequence or acknowledgement numbers and no connection
state, so at the transport layer there is nothing that lets us distinguish a
retransmission from a new datagram. Any such detection would need
application-layer information, which this tool does not inspect.

Payload location
----------------
libpcap hands each packet to the callback in the same reusable buffer, so the
printed payload address is the fixed offset of the payload inside that buffer
(it repeats across packets by design).

Note on IPv6: the transport header is read from the first Next Header field;
packets whose TCP/UDP header sits behind IPv6 extension headers are treated as
"other" and skipped. The provided capture is IPv4-only, but the IPv6 path is
implemented.
